(vi) The multiplicity of statute and fora in the regime prior to the IBC led to a framework for
insolvency and bankruptcy which was inadequate and ineffective, and resulted in undue delay;
(vii) The underlying purpose and object of enacting the IBC was to ensure a timely resolution of
insolvency and bankruptcy which would:
(a) Maximize of the value of assets;
(b) Promote entrepreneurship;
(c) Facilitate the availability of credit;
(d) Support the development of credit markets; and
(e) Balance interests of all stake-holders.
(viii) Bearing the above aspects in mind, the IBC, which is a consolidating and
amending statute, came to be enacted; and
(ix) The IBC, in a clear departure from the past, separates commercial aspects of
insolvency and bankruptcy proceedings from judicial aspects.
